WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.220
Under the best laboratory conditions, one E. coli cell can split into two about

2
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:14.520
every 20 minutes. A cold sore can heal, then come back later in about the same spot.

3
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.300
How do bacteria and viruses make more of themselves, and why do some infections come

4
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.300
back years later?

5
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:24.753
Most bacteria reproduce by binary fission.

6
00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:29.293
One cell grows, copies its DNA, and splits into two cells.

7
00:00:30.173 --> 00:00:34.453
Each new cell gets a complete copy of the chromosome, so the two are usually

8
00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:37.153
identical. Conjugation is different.

9
00:00:38.053 --> 00:00:42.533
One cell passes a plasmid, a small extra circle of DNA, to another.

10
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:45.713
There are still two cells, but one has new genes.

11
00:00:46.783 --> 00:00:50.123
Doubling time is how long a population takes to double.

12
00:00:51.023 --> 00:00:55.423
Take a model population of 100 cells that doubles every 20 minutes.

13
00:00:56.223 --> 00:01:00.583
Two hours is six doublings, so 100 cells become 6,400.

14
00:01:01.723 --> 00:01:07.423
From one E. coli cell, 21 doublings, or seven hours, give more than two million

15
00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:11.003
cells. These are calculated numbers, not counts.

16
00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:15.577
The model assumes no cells die, no waiting, and endless food.

17
00:01:16.537 --> 00:01:17.537
Real cultures differ.

18
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:20.857
First comes a lag, when the count barely rises.

19
00:01:21.777 --> 00:01:24.197
Then comes fast growth, close to steady doubling.

20
00:01:25.137 --> 00:01:28.497
Then food runs low and wastes build up, so growth levels off.

21
00:01:29.277 --> 00:01:33.277
If the culture is left long enough, the number of living cells can start to fall.

22
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.430
A virus cannot split, grow, or make anything on its own.

23
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.910
It needs a host cell, the living cell it infects.

24
00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.110
A bacteriophage, or phage, infects bacteria.

25
00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.630
In the lytic cycle, the phage injects its DNA.

26
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.610
The host's machinery, helped by some proteins made from phage genes, copies it and

27
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.610
builds viral parts.

28
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.690
New phages are assembled.

29
00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.770
The cell bursts, the new phages escape, and the host cell dies.

30
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:07.210
In the lysogenic cycle, the viral DNA stays in the host and is copied with it.

31
00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.430
In phage lambda, it joins the host's own DNA.

32
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.470
This stored DNA is called a prophage.

33
00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.650
Each time the bacterium divides, both daughter cells get a copy.

34
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.130
Later, damage to the cell's DNA can switch the prophage into the lytic cycle.

35
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:29.370
The herpes virus behind cold sores also stays in sensory nerve cells, and from time

36
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.210
to time becomes active again.

37
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.290
A virus never divides.

38
00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.390
A host cell builds it, right away or later.
